# **The movie where Zeke stops being able to pick things up**, and the only input
# that shows the *player* on the wrong side of `object_collide`'s third exit.
#
# BLHR is level 45. This is the chained route -- collect the type-$38 object at
# (540,1100) first, then carry on to the type-$36 one at (866,994) -- and it
# collects neither, because level 45's spiders **pick him up and carry him**. His
# own display record leaves the list, and when it comes back his
# `ACTOR_COLLIDE_ID` reads **$38** rather than $05.
#
# $38 is not one of the three ids `$80:CAEE` accepts, so he walks over an object
# with both records live, both ids non-zero and different, the pair well inside
# the box -- and the object's handler runs all three `CMP`s, matches none of
# them, and returns carry clear having written nothing. That is
# **`object_ignore`**, and all three of the routine's exits are diffed here:
# `object_taken` (14), `object_spent` (3) and `object_ignore` (**48**).
#
# `movies/level29-fighting.zmv` reaches that third exit too, and more cheaply: a
# shot in flight is id $5C, which is also not one of the three. So this movie is
# not load-bearing for the coverage number. What it is load-bearing for is the
# claim that a *player* can be on that branch.
#
# It is also the largest single-level-45 sample in the corpus -- `zamn_cosim
# verify -f 4400` checks 95,261 calls with no divergence -- and it takes
# `object_spent` and `player_no_effect` three times each, the same way
# `movies/level45-contested.zmv` does. That movie is kept for its own reason: it
# is the one where a monster and the player stand on the same object on the same
# frame, which is a different way to lose an object from this one.
#
# Route: east along the bottom, north up the x=836 lane into the spiders, then
# whatever the fitter could still do with a player who was being carried. The
# stutter of Left/Right lines after frame 4156 is that -- re-planning from a
# position the player does not control.

